 ('The genetic code','Several different mRNA codons specify the same amino acid. Which property of the genetic code does this illustrate?', ['Degeneracy','Universality','An unambiguous code','Non-overlapping reading of codons'], 'Degeneracy means that more than one codon can specify a particular amino acid.','sl'),
 ('Matching an anticodon','An mRNA codon is shown as 5′-GCA-3′. Which anticodon pairs with it when written in the opposite aligned direction?', ['3′-CGU-5′','3′-GCA-5′','3′-CGT-5′','5′-CGU-3′'], 'Complementary RNA pairing is G–C and A–U, with antiparallel codon–anticodon alignment.','ahl'),
 ('Processing a transcript','Which event can produce different mature mRNA molecules from the same primary transcript?', ['Alternative splicing of exons','Copying the same unprocessed transcript into more identical molecules','Using different ribosomes to translate an unchanged mature mRNA','Removing every exon and retaining only the introns'], 'Different exon combinations can be retained during alternative splicing of the primary transcript.','ahl'),
