b.add(c,'Moving climatic ranges','Bird populations on a tropical mountain occur at higher mean elevations over successive surveys.',parts=[P('Explain how warming could produce this shift.',R('Suitable temperature conditions may move upslope.'),R('Survival, reproduction or colonization can favour populations at higher elevations.')),P('Explain why movement upslope is not by itself evidence of genetic adaptation.',R('Range redistribution can occur without heritable changes in the birds; genetic / inheritance evidence is required.')),P('State one risk for species already near the summit.',A('Available suitable habitat may shrink or disappear because there is little higher ground.'))],notes='New Guinea upland-bird example can be discussed; this question makes no claim to measured source data.')
b.add(c,'Upwelling and food webs','Climate-related changes alter nutrient-rich upwelling in an ocean region.',parts=[P('Explain how reduced nutrient supply can affect marine food webs.',R('Lower nutrient availability can limit phytoplankton primary production.'),R('Less biomass / energy is then available to support higher trophic levels.')),P('State why temperature alone may not predict the whole ecosystem response.',R('Nutrients, mixing, oxygen, species interactions and timing can also change and influence production.'))])
b.add(c,'More insect generations','In a constructed model for spruce bark beetles, warmer growing seasons shorten development time within a viable temperature range.',level='ahl',parts=[P('Explain how this can increase pressure on host trees.',R('More generations / faster reproduction can increase beetle numbers during a season.'),R('Greater infestation can damage more trees and increase mortality.')),P('Explain why indefinite extrapolation to ever-higher temperature is invalid.',R('Development and survival have upper thermal limits; extreme heat or other constraints may reduce populations.'))],notes='Required spruce bark beetle example; no unsupported universal warming benefit.')
